---
// src/components/Header.astro — "The Horizon" (v5)
// The header is now an observation, not an illustration:
//
//   1. REAL TIME — the sky, sun, and horizon key to the visitor's clock,
//      and now to the visitor's actual SOLAR DAY: sunrise/sunset/solar noon
//      for Lubbock are computed from the date (NOAA-style formula), so the
//      dawn/dusk windows and the sun's height track the real season instead
//      of fixed clock hours. Four phases persist: dawn, day, dusk, night.
//      (Precise wording, since it matters for what a visitor is looking at:
//      every celestial value here is computed for LUBBOCK's coordinates,
//      not the visitor's. Someone loading the site from Berlin at their own
//      local noon sees the Llano Estacado's night sky. That's deliberate —
//      the site is about West Texas, so the header shows West Texas's sky —
//      but it's the visitor's *clock instant*, not their location, that
//      feeds in.)
//   2. PER-PAGE GEOGRAPHY — each route carries its own silhouette in the
//      middle distance: one broad mesa for home, the true flat Llano for
//      cv, a jagged double-summit stacked mesa for publications, a
//      plateau-rim-apron Caprock profile for photography. The line morphs
//      between them on navigation (CSS `d` transition; instant swap where
//      unsupported). A far ground plane morphs behind them on the same
//      transition — very low relief, genuinely flat on cv — and the ground
//      itself is a top-to-bottom gradient rather than a flat cutout. (That
//      replaced a fixed jagged "distant ridge"; see note 12.)
//   3. ONE OBJECT ON THE LINE — an Aermotor windmill, 1px strokes. Its
//      sails turn on a 22s baseline, nudged faster or slower by the actual
//      current wind speed at Lubbock. Stands left of solar noon and shows
//      at every width; frozen under prefers-reduced-motion. (As of the
//      azimuth work below, the sun now travels PAST it rather than sitting
//      permanently to its right — see note 8.)
//   4. REAL WEATHER — one fetch (re-checked at most every 30 minutes) now
//      drives the windmill's speed, the dust haze thickness along the
//      line, AND (2026-08-02) the intensity of the grazing-light effect
//      below — real crepuscular rays need particulates to scatter
//      visibly, so a dusty/windy dawn now looks more dramatic than a
//      clear/calm one, driven by the same wind fetch. A calm day shows
//      almost none of the haze; a windy one shows more of it. (Clouds
//      were tried here too — a set of soft drifting shapes keyed to real
//      cloud cover/wind — but they read as the weakest, most decorative
//      layer on the header and were cut in review; the fetch no longer
//      requests cloud cover at all.)
//   5. THE NIGHT SKY — the moon renders its real current phase (waxing/
//      waning crescent via a masked second disc) AND only appears when a
//      real moonrise/moonset calculation says it's actually above the
//      horizon — some nights it's just a genuinely real star field, same
//      as the real sky would look. (Reworked 2026-08-02: the old fixed
//      20-dot scatter — same every night, no relation to real astronomy —
//      is now a real bright-star catalog (26 at first, 77 as of the
//      2026-08-10 expansion), each with real RA/Dec, positioned by
//      actual altitude/azimuth for Lubbock at the current moment, same
//      class of math as the moon above. Magnitude drives size/brightness
//      — compressed range, since real stars are point sources and
//      brightness mostly reads as glow, not disc size — and atmospheric
//      extinction dims stars low near the horizon, same physics as the
//      grazing-light color shift. Orion's Belt and the Big Dipper are in
//      the catalog as real stars, not a special-cased overlay — when
//      they're actually up and well-placed, an attentive repeat visitor
//      might notice the shape, same spirit as the crane marker below; no
//      extra code path needed for that. Rendered as small circular divs
//      with a fixed pixel diameter rather than SVG circles in a stretched
//      viewBox, so they stay round regardless of the header's actual
//      proportions on a given viewport.) Neither the moon nor the stars
//      twinkle; the sky changes state, it doesn't animate for its own
//      sake. NOTE: the moon is still pinned at a fixed left/bottom — it
//      is the one sky object whose POSITION isn't real. See note 8.
//      (Superseded twice over: the moon was placed in note 13, and the
//      stars' own invented vertical band went in note 14.)
//   6. GRAZING LIGHT (reworked 2026-08-02, sun-color blend added 2026-08-05)
//      — no longer a symmetric 8-line starburst. Real low-angle light
//      scatters mostly horizontally (more atmosphere in the sightline), so
//      this is: a soft aureole around the sun, a
//      handful of uneven mostly-horizontal rays, a faint rim-light catching
//      the ridge silhouette, and a twilight arch standing on the horizon,
//      brightest toward the sun's bearing (see note 10) —
//      all driven by the same real elevation value as before, all tinted
//      by one continuous amber-to-gold color computed from that same
//      elevation (deep amber right at the horizon, fading to neutral
//      gold by the top of the dawn/dusk window). The sun disc itself now
//      blends that same --grazing-color continuously into its own
//      gradient (scaled by --grazing-light) rather than snapping a fixed
//      hue-rotate on/off at the dawn/dusk phase boundary — so it eases
//      from a deep orange at the horizon into its plain daytime color as
//      it climbs, and back again symmetrically at dusk, with no hard
//      edge at the phase transition. It also still visibly flattens and
//      darkens near the horizon (atmospheric refraction compressing a
//      low sun), relaxing back to a normal circle as it climbs, scaled
//      by the same value. Grazing intensity itself is additionally
//      scaled by the real wind-driven haze strength from #4 above (for
//      the bloom/arch/rays/rim-light — NOT for the disc's own
//      color/flatten, which is a function of elevation alone), and now
//      eases with a lingering hold right at the horizon rather than a
//      straight linear fade — see grazing-light easing note below.
//   7. A SEASONAL MARKER — a small sandhill-crane flock silhouette appears
//      only during the real fall migration window through the Llano
//      Estacado (mid-Oct-mid-Dec). Static, not animated; a detail for a
//      repeat visitor, invisible the rest of the year.
//   8. REAL SOLAR AZIMUTH (2026-08-05) — the sun moves HORIZONTALLY across
//      the header as well as vertically. Until then `#sun-wrap` was pinned
//      at `left: calc(50% - 6px)` and only its `bottom` varied, so the sun
//      rose and set in the identical spot — and the star field (note 5) was
//      already placed by real azimuth, meaning the tiny background objects
//      were more astronomically honest than the largest foreground one.
//      solarPosition() returns the sun's true compass bearing using the same
//      atan2 hour-angle form starAltAz() uses, projected to screen x with
//      the SAME mapping applyStars() uses. Matching the existing projection
//      matters more than picking a "better" one: two different projections
//      in one sky would read worse than either alone. (That principle held
//      for one pass and then quietly broke when the sky window arrived; it
//      is restored properly in note 15.)
//      At 33.58N that yields rise 60.8deg -> 16.9% / noon
//      180deg -> 50% / set 299.2deg -> 83.1% at the summer solstice, and
//      117.9 -> 32.7% / 180 -> 50% / 242.1 -> 67.3% at the winter one — so
//      solar noon stays dead centre (the midday composition is unchanged)
//      and the WIDTH of the daily sweep is itself seasonal, broad in June
//      and visibly pinched in December. #horizon-rim-light and the twilight
//      arch's bright dome track --sun-az too; #sun-bloom and #sun-rays are
//      children of the wrap and follow for free. #sun-wrap precedes
//      #windmill in DOM order, so the windmill correctly occludes the disc
//      as it passes; around a winter sunrise (~32.7%) the two nearly
//      coincide.
//
//   9. REAL SOLAR ALTITUDE (2026-08-05, second pass) — the sun's HEIGHT is
//      now physical too, which it never was. Reported symptom: on a
//      ?debugTime run for 21 Dec the sun was still sitting above the line
//      well after the 17:43 sunset. Cause: mapElevation() ramped --sun-elev
//      linearly across the twilight WINDOW (sunset +/- 35 min) with a floor
//      of 6%, so the sunset instant landed at that window's midpoint and
//      emitted 10% — then the disc vanished at duskEnd instead of setting.
//      Real altitude for that moment was -0.85deg.
//
//      elevationPercent() now maps altitude to --sun-elev with 0deg at
//      exactly 0%, and the CSS pins 0% to the horizon line (a 3px
//      correction on #sun-wrap's `bottom` — see the comment there), so the
//      disc is bisected by the line at true sunset and keeps sinking
//      through it, occluded by the ground band on the way down. The mapping
//      is a ^0.6 curve rather than linear, to expand the low-altitude end
//      where the interesting things happen and to stop a linear map parking
//      the summer sun against the header's ceiling for hours either side of
//      noon.
//
//      Retuned 2026-08-05 (fourth pass) after the December sun read as too
//      low. Two causes, one of them a real error: the curve normalised
//      altitude against 90deg, but the sun at 33.58N never exceeds 79.86deg,
//      so the top ~7% of the range was unreachable and everything under it
//      was squashed to suit. It now normalises against PEAK_ALTITUDE_DEG,
//      derived from the site's latitude, so summer-solstice noon lands
//      exactly at the top of the span. SKY_SPAN_PCT also went 34 -> 46,
//      lifting the whole arc. Solar noon now renders at 27.1% in December
//      and 46% in June, against 18.6% / 31.7% before and a flat 36%
//      year-round in the original code.
//
//      Net effect on the code: this pass DELETED solarTimesUTCMinutes(),
//      computeSolarState(), mapElevation(), solarAzimuthDeg(), normMin(),
//      inWindow(), windowLength() and TWILIGHT_MIN, replacing all of them
//      with one solarPosition() + two small pure functions. The phase now
//      comes from real altitude against civil twilight (+/-6deg) instead of
//      clock windows, which is why the 2026-08-04 wraparound bug below is
//      marked superseded rather than merely fixed.
//
//  10. THE TWILIGHT ARCH (2026-08-05, seventh pass) — #sun-streak was cut
//      and its role folded into #haze-warm-overlay. The streak drew a
//      horizontal colour band at the SUN'S ALTITUDE, which isn't a real
//      sunset phenomenon: what sits at the sun's altitude is the parhelic
//      circle (white, ice-crystal, rare) and sun dogs (two discrete spots
//      22deg out, not a wash). The broad horizontal colour in a real sunset
//      is the twilight arch, and it hugs the HORIZON rather than rising with
//      the sun. The error was partly self-concealing — near the horizon the
//      two lines nearly coincide, and the streak faded as they diverged —
//      but it was the one element in the grazing-light set that named
//      nothing real, and it overlapped territory the bloom and the haze tint
//      already held. The overlay now carries a sun-centred dome over a
//      horizon-wide wash: the actual phenomenon, with a falloff that is
//      symmetric about the sun instead of anchored to the viewport edges,
//      and one fewer layer. See the long note on #haze-warm-overlay in the
//      stylesheet.
//
//  11. THE AUREOLE (2026-08-05, eighth pass) — #sun-bloom kept, reshaped.
//      It names real physics (Mie forward-scattering, which is why it grows
//      as the sun drops and why --grazing-effective is the right driver), but
//      it was drawn as a 46x18 horizontal bar on the mistaken reasoning that
//      scatter spreads sideways. An aureole is circularly symmetric about the
//      sun; the horizontal look in photographs comes from the glow merging
//      into the bright horizon band below it. The twilight arch (note 10) now
//      supplies exactly that, so the stretch had become double-counting.
//      Now near-round, and gained two real dependencies it was missing: its
//      SIZE scales with --haze-strength (aureole radius grows with aerosol
//      loading, so a dusty day gets a bigger glow and not just a brighter
//      one), and it shares --horizon-flatten with the disc, since refraction
//      compresses everything in that sightline — at sunset you previously had
//      a squashed sun inside a perfectly round glow.
//
//  12. THE FAR GROUND PLANE (2026-08-05) — the fixed "distant ridge" is gone.
//      It rendered wrong (translucent, so the sun and its glows showed
//      through land), it was geographically false (there is no distant ridge
//      visible from the Llano Estacado — the Caprock is a drop-off fifty
//      miles east, not a range on the skyline), and it contradicted the
//      silhouettes' own argument, since the cv route's dead-flat Llano had an
//      invented range floating above it. Replaced by a per-route far ground
//      plane 0.7-2.2 units above the baseline that morphs on the same `d`
//      transition and goes genuinely flat on cv. Depth here comes from
//      atmosphere, not relief — which the dust haze and its aerial-
//      perspective coupling already provide.
//
//      Two fixes came with it. The plane is fully OPAQUE, with aerial
//      perspective done by pushing its FILL toward the sky colour as dust
//      rises rather than by fading its opacity. And horizonLiftPx() now
//      samples both profiles and takes the higher of the two — sampling only
//      the near silhouette meant that where the near ground was low and the
//      far land rose above it, the sun came to rest at a point the far land
//      already covered.
//
//  13. THE LAST UNREALITIES (2026-08-05). Six things, roughly in order of how
//      much they change what you see.
//
//      THE MOON IS PLACED. It had real phase and a real up/down check but was
//      pinned at left: 61% / bottom: 56% — a spot corresponding to nothing,
//      and the last object in this header sitting somewhere invented. It now
//      goes through the same sky window, elevation curve and terrain lift as
//      the sun. Two things came with it that read more strongly than the
//      position: the TERMINATOR now tilts, because the lit limb always faces
//      the sun and a purely horizontal mask offset meant every crescent stood
//      upright instead of lying on its back the way a low one does out here;
//      and the moon is no longer hidden outside the night phase, because a
//      real moon is up in the daytime sky about half the month and that
//      answer was already being computed and thrown away.
//
//      THE BELT OF VENUS. Opposite the sun after sunset: Earth's own shadow
//      rising blue-grey out of the far horizon with a pink backscatter band
//      sitting on it. The anti-solar point is the sun's azimuth + 180 and its
//      altitude is the negation of the sun's, so the whole structure rises at
//      exactly the rate the sun sinks. The header had a warm arch on the
//      sun's side and nothing at all on the other.
//
//      STAR COLOUR. All of them rendered pure white, which is the one thing stars
//      visibly are not. Each catalog entry gained a B-V index mapped through
//      a seven-stop ramp, blended toward white for dim stars since colour
//      vision fades at low light.
//
//      REFRACTION. The atmosphere lifts everything near the horizon by about
//      34 arcminutes — slightly more than the sun's own diameter, and the
//      reason you watch it touch down when it is geometrically already below.
//      Applied to position and visibility, not to phase, since civil twilight
//      is defined on the geometric centre.
//
//      WINDMILL YAW. An Aermotor's head turns to face the wind; that's what
//      the tail is for. wind_direction_10m is back in the fetch (it was
//      dropped when the cloud layer was cut). Stylized rather than a true
//      projection — see the note in applyWeather.
//
//      THE STATION READOUT. The plate under the name had been an empty
//      triangle since the place/elevation label was cut, and .station-meta had
//      been a dead selector for several passes. It now carries Lubbock's local
//      time and the current wind. This is the smallest change here and
//      probably the most important: every system above is computed from real
//      astronomy and real weather, and until now a visitor had no way of
//      knowing that — it all read as decoration. It also settles an ambiguity
//      that had gone undocumented, that the sky is LUBBOCK's and not the
//      viewer's.
//
//  14. THE STAR FIELD JOINS THE SKY (2026-08-07). Four fixes; the first is
//      visible immediately and the last changes the composition.
//
//      THE DAWN MOON. A leftover selector from the deleted phase-opacity
//      rules left `#horizon-header[data-phase="dawn"] #horizon-moon` sharing
//      the `::after` mask's declaration block. At (2,1,0) it outranked the
//      moon's own rule, so through every dawn the moon took `inset: 0`,
//      `background: var(--sky-horizon)` and the mask's `translateX` — a flat
//      sky-coloured 11px disc pinned to the top-left corner, its box-shadow
//      glow still attached, its gradient and its rotation gone. It read as
//      "the moon has set," which is exactly why it survived several passes.
//      One comma deleted.
//
//      THE PRE-PAINT FLASH. The is:inline script sets data-phase, but since
//      the dark-sky pass the phase supplies only the sky's HUE — --night
//      supplies the darkness. So the script had quietly stopped doing the one
//      job it exists for: a midnight visitor got a pale canvas sky with dark
//      ink until the module landed. It now writes --night and --ink-flip too,
//      using the same smoothstep and the same 0.625 flip point as the module.
//      Retune the two together.
//
//      EXTINCTION. Was `0.35 + 0.65 * min(1, alt/20)` — linear in altitude
//      with a hard floor, so a star sitting ON the horizon still rendered at
//      a third brightness. That floor is why the field needed an invented
//      vertical band to keep it off the land in the first place. Now
//      exponential in airmass, 10^(-0.4*k*(X-1)), with k rising as dust does,
//      so the low sky empties out on its own and a blowing night eats the low
//      stars first — off the same aerosol loading that drives the haze, the
//      aureole and the reddening.
//
//      AND THE STARS ARE PLACED LIKE EVERYTHING ELSE. They were on a
//      top-anchored band of their own, `8 + (1 - alt/60) * 55`, which put a
//      star at 2deg altitude around 61% down from the top — floating in open
//      sky while the moon at 2deg rested on the ridge. They now go through
//      refraction, elevationPercent() and --horizon-lift, the same three the
//      sun and the moon do, and are bottom-anchored with the same +3px
//      centring correction.
//
//      CONSEQUENCE WORTH KNOWING, because it is a composition change and not
//      just a correctness one: the celestial dome from 0-80deg lives in the
//      bottom SKY_SPAN_PCT (46%) of the header, which is the same vertical
//      band the name and the nav occupy. The sun avoids them horizontally via
//      the sky window; the stars cover the full compass and therefore cannot.
//      So the field now sits ACROSS the type rather than above it. Two things
//      keep that from being a mess — #night-sky precedes the content in DOM
//      order, so the type and the land paint in front, and extinction now
//      empties the lowest few degrees on its own — but if it reads as noise,
//      SKY_SPAN_PCT is the dial, and a star-only ceiling below the shared one
//      is the fallback (at the cost of the single curve this pass bought).
//      Two catalog stars, Vega and Pollux, transit above PEAK_ALTITUDE_DEG
//      and will sit pinned at the top of the span around culmination:
//      accepted deliberately, since one curve shared by every body beats a
//      second curve that agrees with nothing.
//
//  15. ONE PROJECTION (2026-08-07). The sun and moon mapped 55-305deg into
//      the measured sky window while the stars mapped the full compass across
//      the full width — two skies disagreeing about where east is. That was
//      defensible only while the two were never on screen together, which is
//      what the old comment argued; the moon stopped being night-only in note
//      13, so they are, all night, every night.
//
//      azimuthToPercent() is now piecewise and shared by every body. The
//      southern arc 55-305deg maps into the measured window exactly as
//      before — so the sun and the moon are unchanged to the pixel and solar
//      noon is still 50% — and the remaining 110deg of northern sky is spread
//      across the two margins, with 0deg and 360deg meeting at the header's
//      edges. That is where due north belongs: the viewer faces south, so the
//      wrap point is directly behind them.
//
//      Continuous and monotonic. The cost is that the horizontal scale
//      changes at 55deg and at 305deg, so a wide constellation straddling one
//      of those bearings is slightly kinked. The Big Dipper is the only thing
//      in this catalog wide enough to show it, and it will sometimes sit
//      right on the northwestern seam. The alternatives were worse: dropping
//      the window puts a summer sunrise back out in a wide screen's margin,
//      and a smooth power curve moves the sun, which has been tuned across
//      eleven passes.
//
//      Two smaller consequences. The moon's rise/set extremes reach about
//      54.8deg here, marginally outside the window — it used to clamp there
//      and now travels a fraction of a percent into the left margin, which is
//      more correct. And the northern segments are only as wide as the
//      margins the layout leaves, so on a narrow viewport with almost no
//      margin the northern sky compresses hard; nothing the sun or moon ever
//      touches is affected.
//
//  16. THE WINDMILL LOOKS AT ITSELF (2026-08-09). Every system in this header
//      had been checked against the thing it names except the one object
//      standing on the line, which was still drawn from memory.
//
//      THE TAIL WAS FORESHORTENED BACKWARDS, and this is the real bug rather
//      than a drawing complaint. #windmill-tail was a CHILD of
//      #windmill-head, so it inherited the rotor's |cos psi|. But the rotor
//      disc's normal points into the wind while the vane's plane CONTAINS
//      the wind - the two foreshorten as |cos psi| and |sin psi|,
//      complementary, never together. The vane was therefore at full length
//      under a due-south wind, the exact case where it points straight away
//      from the viewer and should be a hairline. --head-flip's sign was
//      inverted on top of that: facing south, east is on the LEFT, so an
//      easterly turns the wheel left and trails the vane RIGHT, and the old
//      test sent it left. Both survived because there was no ?debugWindDir -
//      the yaw was the one real-data-driven element in this header that
//      could not be driven by hand. There is one now.
//
//      THE WHEEL WAS A PINWHEEL. Eight spokes through a single centre point
//      is a compass rose. An Aermotor wheel is an ANNULUS: outer rim, inner
//      hub ring, and short pitched sails spanning between them, none of them
//      pointing at the centre. That band is the entire silhouette - it is
//      what separates a windmill from a wagon wheel at distance. Twelve
//      sails at 26deg of lead, not eighteen, because the rim spacing at 22px
//      is already about 2.1 units.
//
//      THE WHEEL ALSO SAT INSIDE THE TOWER. Hub at y=8, radius 5.2, tower
//      top at y=9 - so the lower half of the wheel hung four units below the
//      top girt, in front of nothing. Hub moves to y=6, tower top to y=10.5,
//      and the wheel clears. transform-origin follows it to 11px 6px in
//      three places, which is the one thing here that breaks the yaw
//      silently if it's missed.
//
//      Plus a tail boom and a vane sized like a real one (about half the
//      wheel's diameter), X-bracing across four panels instead of two ladder
//      rungs, a gearbox, a pump rod, and a stroke-weight hierarchy in place
//      of a flat 1px.
//
//      STILL STYLIZED, deliberately: the wheel is ~37% of the tower's height
//      against a real ~24%, because a proportional wheel at this size is six
//      pixels of grey. The ceiling on further detail is the render size (22px
//      / 28px), not the drawing - it's vector, so those two numbers are the
//      dial if the lattice ever wants to be legible.
//
//      NOT DONE, and the obvious next one: FURLING. Past roughly 35mph an
//      Aermotor swings its vane parallel to the wheel and turns edge-on to
//      the wind, and stops - that's how it survives a Llano spring. Right now
//      high wind just spins it faster, which is the one thing a real mill
//      won't do. DUST_SATURATION_MPH is already 36.

//  17. THE CATALOG GROWS, CAREFULLY (2026-08-10). The field read as sparse:
//      26 catalog entries means about 10 above the horizon after extinction,
//      one star per ~1,300px of a band that on a phone is only ~359x37px.
//
//      The obvious fix — extend to a magnitude limit — is the wrong one, and
//      the numbers say why. mag<=4 is 513 entries: ~200 visible, an 8px
//      spacing in a 37px-tall band, 15KB of catalog shipped to every visitor,
//      and 400 querySelector calls per applyStars() pass through
//      horizonLiftPx(). Worse, it would bury the point of having a real
//      catalog at all: Orion's Belt and the Big Dipper are in here as real
//      stars precisely so an attentive repeat visitor might catch the shape,
//      and drowning them in mag-4 filler destroys exactly that.
//
//      So this is CURATED to 77, not magnitude-limited. Every addition either
//      completes a figure someone can actually name (Orion's shoulders and
//      sword, the Scorpius sting, the Sagittarius teapot, the Northern Cross,
//      the Great Square, the Cassiopeia W, the Leo sickle) or anchors a
//      stretch of sky that was otherwise empty. Result: ~30 visible, ~21px
//      spacing, +1.5KB, 60 querySelectors per pass — comfortably under the
//      point where the `d`-string caching deliberately avoided in
//      horizonLiftPx() would become necessary.
//
//      TWO CONSTRAINTS SHAPED THE LIST. Nothing below dec -56.42deg is
//      included because it never rises at this latitude: Achernar, Rigil
//      Kentaurus and Acrux are all excluded on that basis, and Canopus (dec
//      -52.7, max altitude 3.7deg) survives from the original list mostly as
//      a curiosity, since extinction eats it almost immediately. And the
//      northern sky is now the WORST place to add: note 15's piecewise
//      projection puts the two margins at the header's edges, and the mobile
//      widening in the sky-window pass compressed them to ~16px each, so
//      Cassiopeia is squeezed on a phone and reads properly only on desktop.
//      Polaris is deliberately ABSENT for the same reason taken to its
//      limit: at azimuth 0 it maps to exactly x=0 in every layout and would
//      sit permanently half-clipped against the left edge.
//
//      NOT DONE, deliberately: no procedural or invented filler stars. The
//      whole claim of this layer is that every point is a real star at its
//      real bearing, and one fake dot forfeits it.
//
//  18. THE WHEEL GETS A THIRD DIMENSION (2026-08-19). The wheel was drawn as
//      a FLAT DISC: rim, hub ring and sails all concentric on (11,6), the
//      whole assembly foreshortened by one shared scaleX. A real Aermotor
//      wheel is a shallow DISH, concave into the wind, and the sails are
//      pitched OUT of its plane - that pitch is the entire difference
//      between a fan and a wheel. Neither fact was in the drawing, so no
//      matter how far the head yawed the wheel stayed a squashed circle.
//
//      Per-sail modelling is not the answer and was rejected: at the largest
//      breakpoint a sail renders about 5.5 x 1.0px (28px svg / 22 units,
//      x1.25 --windmill-scale, x1.3 --wheel-scale = 2.07 px/unit), under up
//      to 1.2px of dust blur at 0.55 opacity. Light and shade INSIDE a sail
//      lands below one pixel and reads as a rendering fault, not as form -
//      and it would put filled mass in a header made of line work. Worse,
//      the rotor spins: tone drawn into the sails rotates WITH them, when
//      real light is fixed in space and the sails pass through it.
//
//      So the dimension is carried by the wheel as a whole, in two parts.
//
//      THE HUB IS SET BACK FROM THE RIM. Everything on the back plane -
//      gearbox, hub ring, hub cap - moved into #windmill-hub, which
//      translates by --hub-offset toward the tail (downwind, away from the
//      concave face). Zero face-on, largest edge-on. That lateral parallax
//      between rim and hub is the depth cue, and it is the one that survives
//      at 17px because it operates on the whole object rather than inside a
//      sail. The translate is divided by the rim's own scale in JS, because
//      it sits INSIDE #windmill-head's scaleX and would otherwise be crushed
//      exactly when it is needed most.
//
//      THE SAILS NO LONGER COLLAPSE WITH THE RIM. The 26deg of lead in the
//      sail paths is IN-PLANE skew; a real sail is also tilted out of the
//      plane, so as the wheel turns edge-on the rim shrinks toward a line
//      while the sails rotate toward presenting their faces. #windmill-rotor
//      (sails only now) sits inside a new #windmill-sail-plane carrying
//      --sail-spread, so the sail band stays wider than the rim ellipse -
//      about 15% at 45deg of yaw, 25% at 60deg, and floored so the band
//      never becomes a hairline. The rim circle came out of the rotor
//      entirely: it is centred on the axis, so spinning it was a no-op, and
//      keeping it there forced it to share the sails' foreshortening.
//
//      STILL A STYLIZED PROJECTION, and note 16's caveat stands. A scaleX is
//      not the true projection of a pitched surface - it reproduces the
//      silhouette's envelope and distorts inside it. At this size the
//      envelope is all that resolves.
//
//      NOT DONE: giving the RIM visible thickness. A rim hoop's axial depth
//      is a few inches on an 8-foot wheel, genuinely sub-pixel here, and
//      faking it larger reads as a drawing error rather than as depth.

// The benchmark plate under the name no longer carries a place/elevation
// label — decided it was redundant with the footer's own elevation rail
// and cut it; the small survey mark stays as the landmark's identity.
// (Superseded by note 13: the plate carries a live readout again.)
//
// Also folded in from review: the frost is its own layer (opacity +
// backdrop-filter animate; gradients never snap), the scroll listener
// attaches once and only where scroll-timeline is unavailable, and
// active states + silhouettes refresh on astro:after-swap (the persisted
// header no longer goes stale on navigation).
//
// Shared-element transition (2026-07-27): three elements carry
// transition:name so a soft-navigation view transition from the lab
// traverse page morphs directly into this header — the signature
// watermark into the real signature, the descending page's name into
// the landmark name, and the elevation readout into the benchmark
// station plate.
//
// 2026-08-04 fix — SUPERSEDED, kept for the history. day→dusk→night was
// skipping straight to night. Root cause was that solarTimesUTCMinutes()
// (and its inline pre-paint twin) computed sunriseUTC/sunsetUTC using
// `720 - 4 * lon - eqtime`, and for a negative (western) longitude like
// Lubbock's -101.8552 that `-4 * lon` term adds roughly +407 minutes —
// pushing sunsetUTC past 1440 in summer (it lands around 1540-1550). But
// `nowUTCmin` was a raw getUTCHours()*60 + getUTCMinutes(), which resets to
// 0 at UTC midnight — only ~7pm local in Lubbock during summer DST, well
// before the ~8:15pm local sunset. So around 7pm local, `now` wrapped back
// near 0 while duskStart/duskEnd sat up near 1505/1580, no window matched,
// and everything fell through to night. It was fixed at the time with
// circular normMin()/inWindow() comparisons.
//
// That whole mechanism is now GONE (see note 9): phase comes from the sun's
// real altitude, and nothing in this component compares clock times against
// window bounds any more, so this class of bug can't recur.
//
// 2026-08-05: the sun disc's dawn/dusk color used a fixed hue-rotate(-12deg)
// / hue-rotate(8deg) gated entirely by [data-phase="dawn"/"dusk"], so it
// snapped on/off at the phase boundary instead of easing — intensity scaled
// smoothly via --grazing-light, but the effect's very existence didn't.
// Replaced with a continuous color-mix of --grazing-color (the same deep-
// amber-to-gold value already driving the bloom/streak/rim-light) directly
// into the disc's own gradient stops, scaled by --grazing-light — so the
// same deep orange now eases in at the horizon and fades back to the plain
// daytime gradient by ~16% elevation, in both directions, with no phase-
// boundary snap. The two phase-scoped filter overrides are gone; brightness
// still ride --grazing-light exactly as before, just no longer duplicated
// per-phase. (The horizon-flatten squash rode --grazing-light too until the
// 2026-08-05 golden-hour retune moved it onto its own --horizon-flatten, on
// a tighter altitude range — see REFRACTION_FLATTEN_DEG.) Also eased --grazing-light
// itself off a straight linear fade: a smoothstep-shaped curve now holds
// close to full intensity for the first stretch right at the horizon
// before easing out, so the deep-orange moment reads as a held beat rather
// than an instant that's already fading the moment it appears.
/* THE SIGNATURE JOINS THE SKY (2026-08-17). Imported as a raw string
   rather than through astro:assets, so the mark ships as inline SVG and
   can inherit `currentColor` from CSS. As an <Image> it was an opaque
   <img>, recolourable only by filter — which is why it had been stuck on
   invert() while every other object in this header derived its appearance
   from the real sky.

   The asset already declares fill="currentColor" on its root, so inlining
   alone is what makes it live; no per-path rewriting is needed.

   aria-hidden + focusable are injected here rather than edited into the
   asset, so re-exporting the signature can't silently drop them. The
   accessible name lives on the wrapper span instead (see the markup).

   COST, stated plainly: this moves ~31KB of path data out of a separately
   cached request and into the HTML of every page, since the header renders
   on all of them. It gzips to about 14KB, and path data compresses well.
   If that ever matters more than the colour coupling does, the fallback is
   to keep <Image> and accept that the mark and the name disagree at dusk. */
import signatureMarkup from '../assets/signature.svg?raw';
const signatureInline = signatureMarkup.replace(
  '<svg ',
  '<svg aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" '
);

const currentPath = Astro.url.pathname.replace(/\/$/, "") || "/";
const isActive = (path: string) => currentPath === (path.replace(/\/$/, "") || "/");

const navItems = [
  { href: '/cv', label: 'cv' },
  { href: '/publications', label: 'publications' },
  { href: '/photography', label: 'photography' },
];

// ROUTE GEOGRAPHY (rebuilt 2026-08-18 — see the note on landforms below).
//
// All four silhouettes share an identical command structure (M, H, 38xL, H)
// AND identical x positions, so the CSS `d` property interpolates them
// cleanly and only the y values differ between routes. The outer runs — 0-250
// and 1020-1440 — stay dead flat on every route on purpose: the name and the
// mile markers stand on those, and type reads badly off a slope.
//
// WHAT CHANGED, AND WHY. The previous set was 13 points per route describing
// symmetric bell curves — a rounded hump for home, two spikes for
// publications, a lopsided dome for photography. That is not what this
// country looks like. Three things were wrong with it as landform:
//
//   1. SYMMETRY. Erosional landforms are not symmetric. The Caprock has a
//      gentle back-slope on its west side and an abrupt face on its east,
//      because that is the direction it retreats. Every profile here now runs
//      long-and-gentle on the left, short-and-steep on the right, which is
//      also the correct compass orientation with north up.
//   2. ROUND TOPS. The single most identifiable feature of this landscape is
//      that the tops are FLAT. A mesa is a piece of the old plain left
//      standing; its summit is the plain. Each route now holds a genuinely
//      level cap — the same y repeated across several points — and reaches it
//      through a short rim break rather than a curve.
//   3. CONVEX SLOPES. The old humps were convex all the way down. Real
//      escarpment slopes are the opposite below the rim: a steep face off the
//      caprock, then a long CONCAVE talus apron that flattens as it merges
//      into the plain. That concavity is most of what makes a drawn skyline
//      read as land rather than as a shape.
//
// The four routes are now four real landforms rather than four abstractions:
//
//   home         — a broad caprock mesa. Long west apron with a shoulder
//                  bench where a resistant ledge crops out, a dead-level cap
//                  bitten by one head-cut notch (a draw working back into the
//                  rim), then a short dissected scarp on the east.
//   cv           — the Llano Estacado, and still the flattest thing here on
//                  purpose. What relief it has is now real instead of jitter:
//                  one imperceptible swell, and a playa — the only place the
//                  land dips BELOW the baseline — with its lunette, the
//                  wind-built rim banked on the basin's east lip.
//   publications — Double Mountain, in Stonewall County: two flat-topped
//                  buttes of nearly equal height standing off the escarpment
//                  with a saddle between them. Replaces a jagged twin-peak
//                  that belonged to a different state.
//   photography  — the canyon rim, and the same PLATEAU / RIM / FACE / APRON
//                  grammar the CaprockFooter uses. Long back-slope, broad
//                  level plateau, a hard rim break where the thin caliche cap
//                  fails, the sandstone bench below it, a concave apron, and
//                  one detached outlier butte stranded out on the plain —
//                  which is what a retreating escarpment leaves behind.
//
// The fine relief on the slopes (roughly a fifth of a unit) is deterministic,
// not random, and it is masked off the caps and benches so those stay
// perfectly level. It reads as gullying at desktop widths and disappears into
// the line on a phone, which is the correct behaviour for both.
//
// SCALE HONESTY: the viewBox is 22 units tall against 1440 wide and renders
// with preserveAspectRatio="none", so all of this is enormously
// vertically exaggerated and horizontally elastic. These are silhouettes in
// the sense a woodcut is — the grammar is accurate, the proportions are not.
const SILHOUETTES: Record<string, string> = {
  home:         "M0 19 H250 L280 18.63 L300 18.53 L320 18.13 L340 17.74 L360 17.33 L380 16.92 L400 16.62 L420 16.26 L440 15.89 L460 15.56 L480 15.4 L500 14.39 L520 13.55 L540 12.52 L560 11.4 L580 10.81 L600 9.75 L620 9.5 L640 9.5 L660 9.5 L680 9.5 L700 9.92 L720 10.31 L740 9.81 L760 9.57 L780 9.54 L800 9.5 L820 11.71 L840 13.78 L860 15.21 L880 16.5 L900 17.12 L920 17.64 L940 17.88 L960 18.29 L980 18.75 L1000 18.86 L1020 19 H1440",
  cv:           "M0 19 H250 L280 18.99 L300 18.95 L320 18.89 L340 18.88 L360 18.89 L380 18.85 L400 18.85 L420 18.84 L440 18.78 L460 18.76 L480 18.81 L500 18.82 L520 18.84 L540 18.91 L560 18.91 L580 18.89 L600 18.92 L620 18.99 L640 19.08 L660 19.17 L680 19.2 L700 19.18 L720 19.19 L740 19.11 L760 18.99 L780 18.96 L800 18.78 L820 18.64 L840 18.73 L860 18.87 L880 18.87 L900 18.9 L920 18.95 L940 18.97 L960 18.99 L980 19 L1000 18.99 L1020 19 H1440",
  publications: "M0 19 H250 L280 18.53 L300 18.27 L320 18.18 L340 17.64 L360 17.21 L380 16.88 L400 15.95 L420 15.03 L440 13.46 L460 11.45 L480 9.71 L500 8.2 L520 8.2 L540 8.2 L560 8.2 L580 8.2 L600 9.91 L620 11.93 L640 13.59 L660 15.29 L680 16.03 L700 16.8 L720 16.55 L740 15.67 L760 14.14 L780 12.12 L800 10.04 L820 8.8 L840 8.8 L860 8.8 L880 8.8 L900 10.59 L920 12.7 L940 14.51 L960 16.63 L980 17.73 L1000 18.52 L1020 19 H1440",
  photography:  "M0 19 H250 L280 18.04 L300 17.6 L320 17.23 L340 16.48 L360 15.61 L380 14.83 L400 14.01 L420 13.09 L440 12.34 L460 11.64 L480 10.72 L500 9.84 L520 9.47 L540 8.67 L560 8.6 L580 8.6 L600 8.6 L620 8.6 L640 8.6 L660 8.6 L680 8.6 L700 8.6 L720 8.6 L740 11.18 L760 13.23 L780 14.08 L800 14.12 L820 14.15 L840 15.51 L860 16.91 L880 17.58 L900 18.31 L920 17.82 L940 16.86 L960 16.7 L980 16.7 L1000 18.44 L1020 19 H1440",
};

// THE FAR GROUND PLANE (2026-08-05, replaces the distant ridge).
//
// What was here: a fixed, non-morphing backdrop RIDGE at y 10-15 — a jagged
// range standing 7-12px above the baseline, identical on every route. Three
// things were wrong with it, in ascending order.
//
// It rendered wrong: at ~0.58 opacity the sun's disc AND its 34px box-shadow
// glows showed straight through it. Land is opaque; anything you can see the
// sun through reads as being behind the sun, so it stopped looking like land.
//
// It was geographically false: Lubbock sits on the Llano Estacado, one of the
// flattest surfaces in North America. There is no distant ridge visible from
// there — the Caprock is a drop-off some fifty miles east, not a range rising
// above the horizon. On a site whose whole subject is this landscape, that's
// the wrong kind of wrong.
//
// And it contradicted the silhouettes' own argument. The cv route is the true
// flat Llano, and the point of that near-zero relief is that it reads as flat
// BECAUSE the others aren't — but a fixed range floated above it across the
// full width, so the one page built to say "this place is featureless" was
// the page where the invented mountains showed most.
//
// What replaces it is a far ground plane: a second, much lower profile
// sitting only 0.65-2.2 units above the baseline, per route, morphing with
// the same `d` transition as the near silhouette. It gives the layering cue
// without inventing terrain, and it goes genuinely flat on cv.
//
// 2026-08-18: rebuilt alongside the near profiles, on the same x grid. The
// governing rule is now explicit — THE FAR PLANE IS NEVER A SMALL COPY OF THE
// NEAR ONE. It was, and that read as one hill with a shadow. Distance on this
// plain does not look like a smaller hill; it looks like a second dead-level
// line at a different height, because what you are actually seeing fifty
// miles out is the same flat surface. So each far plane is a long level
// table with soft ends, placed to show in the gaps the near landform leaves:
// on both flanks of home's mesa, past the toe of publications' aprons, and on
// photography as a lower step to the right — the rim continuing away from you
// after the near one has broken down. It stays clear of photography's outlier
// butte by about a unit so the butte still reads as standing in front of it.
//
// Worth knowing: .horizon-ground paints over this, so wherever the near land
// is higher the far plane is simply hidden, and horizonLiftPx() samples BOTH
// and takes whichever is higher — so where the far plane does show above the
// near skyline, the sun correctly sets behind it.
const FAR_PLANES: Record<string, string> = {
  home:         "M0 18.35 H250 L280 18.23 L300 18 L320 17.81 L340 17.73 L360 17.54 L380 17.33 L400 17.22 L420 17.21 L440 17.19 L460 17.15 L480 17.15 L500 17.15 L520 17.15 L540 17.15 L560 17.15 L580 17.15 L600 17.15 L620 17.15 L640 17.15 L660 17.15 L680 17.15 L700 17.15 L720 17.15 L740 17.15 L760 17.15 L780 17.15 L800 17.15 L820 17.15 L840 17.15 L860 17.15 L880 17.15 L900 17.15 L920 17.28 L940 17.49 L960 17.58 L980 17.88 L1000 18.2 L1020 18.35 H1440",
  cv:           "M0 18.35 H250 L280 18.34 L300 18.33 L320 18.32 L340 18.29 L360 18.29 L380 18.31 L400 18.29 L420 18.28 L440 18.29 L460 18.26 L480 18.25 L500 18.27 L520 18.28 L540 18.28 L560 18.28 L580 18.26 L600 18.24 L620 18.24 L640 18.25 L660 18.25 L680 18.27 L700 18.27 L720 18.25 L740 18.25 L760 18.24 L780 18.25 L800 18.28 L820 18.3 L840 18.3 L860 18.31 L880 18.3 L900 18.27 L920 18.3 L940 18.32 L960 18.32 L980 18.34 L1000 18.35 L1020 18.35 H1440",
  publications: "M0 18.35 H250 L280 18.23 L300 18.02 L320 17.92 L340 17.79 L360 17.59 L380 17.45 L400 17.35 L420 17.35 L440 17.35 L460 17.35 L480 17.35 L500 17.35 L520 17.35 L540 17.35 L560 17.35 L580 17.35 L600 17.35 L620 17.35 L640 17.35 L660 17.35 L680 17.35 L700 17.35 L720 17.35 L740 17.35 L760 17.35 L780 17.35 L800 17.35 L820 17.35 L840 17.35 L860 17.35 L880 17.35 L900 17.48 L920 17.71 L940 17.82 L960 17.91 L980 18.12 L1000 18.25 L1020 18.35 H1440",
  photography:  "M0 18.35 H250 L280 18.28 L300 18.12 L320 17.97 L340 17.94 L360 17.83 L380 17.71 L400 17.65 L420 17.62 L440 17.58 L460 17.55 L480 17.5 L500 17.5 L520 17.5 L540 17.5 L560 17.5 L580 17.5 L600 17.5 L620 17.5 L640 17.5 L660 17.5 L680 17.5 L700 17.5 L720 17.5 L740 17.5 L760 17.5 L780 17.65 L800 17.75 L820 17.73 L840 17.7 L860 17.7 L880 17.7 L900 17.7 L920 17.7 L940 17.7 L960 17.7 L980 17.8 L1000 18.2 L1020 18.35 H1440",
};

const routeKey = (p: string): string => {
  const clean = p.replace(/\/$/, "") || "/";
  if (clean.startsWith('/cv')) return 'cv';
  if (clean.startsWith('/publications')) return 'publications';
  if (clean.startsWith('/photography')) return 'photography';
  return 'home';
};

const key = routeKey(currentPath);
const silhouette = SILHOUETTES[key];
const groundPath = `${silhouette} V22 H0 Z`;
const farPlane = FAR_PLANES[key];
const farPlanePath = `${farPlane} V22 H0 Z`;

// 2026-08-04: dev-only time override for testing dawn/dusk/night without
// waiting for the real clock or fighting an OS that auto-resyncs. Gated by
// import.meta.env.DEV (evaluated at build time, so it's compiled out of
// production entirely — not just hidden). The flag is threaded down to
// both the is:inline pre-paint script and the module script via a data
// attribute, since is:inline scripts aren't processed by Vite and can't
// read import.meta.env themselves. When enabled, visiting the page with
// ?debugTime=2026-08-04T20:00:00-05:00 (any ISO string Date() accepts)
// forces every "now" in this component to that timestamp instead of the
// real clock — no system-clock changes, no waiting. ?debugWind=<mph> is the
// companion hook, standing in for the live Open-Meteo reading so the dust
// haze, the aerial perspective it drives, the windmill's speed and the
// dust-reddening of the sunset colour can all be exercised on a still day. As of the azimuth
// work this also drives the sun's HORIZONTAL position, so the same
// override now sweeps the sun across the header, not just up and down —
// which makes the whole arc testable in a few page loads.
const isDev = import.meta.env.DEV;
---

<div class="w-full sticky top-0 z-50 select-none pointer-events-none" transition:persist="horizon-header">
  <header id="horizon-header" class="relative w-full pointer-events-auto overflow-hidden" data-debug-time={isDev ? '1' : undefined}>

    <!-- SKY: base layer, tinted per phase of day -->
    <div id="sky-base" class="absolute inset-0 -z-10 pointer-events-none"></div>
    <!-- SKY: frost layer — its opacity/blur animate; gradients never snap -->
    <div id="sky-frost" class="absolute inset-0 -z-10 pointer-events-none"></div>
    <!-- SKY: evening layer (rises with scroll depth; tint depends on phase) -->
    <div id="sky-dusk" class="absolute inset-0 -z-10 pointer-events-none"></div>
    <!-- SKY: dust haze hugging the line -->
    <div id="horizon-haze" class="absolute bottom-0 left-0 w-full -z-10 pointer-events-none"></div>
    <!-- SKY: THE TWILIGHT ARCH — the warm glow standing on the horizon at
         dawn/dusk, brightest toward the sun's bearing and dimmer along the
         line away from it. Absorbed the old #sun-streak element (see the
         long note on this rule in the stylesheet for why that one went).
         Still a separate overlay rather than a recolouring of #horizon-haze,
         so the haze's own wind-driven opacity/height logic stays untouched. -->
    <div id="haze-warm-overlay" aria-hidden="true" class="absolute bottom-0 left-0 w-full pointer-events-none"></div>
    <!-- SKY: film grain -->
    <div id="sky-grain" class="absolute inset-0 -z-10 pointer-events-none"></div>

    <!-- THE BELT OF VENUS + EARTH'S SHADOW — opposite the sun, not with it:
         a blue-grey band of the planet's own shadow rising out of the far
         horizon with a pink band of backscatter sitting on top of it. Placed
         at the anti-solar azimuth through the same sky window as everything
         else, and rising at exactly the rate the sun sinks. -->
    <div id="belt-of-venus" aria-hidden="true" class="absolute pointer-events-none"></div>

    <!-- GRAZING LIGHT: a faint glow catching the ridge silhouette from
         behind — sits low, near the ground, so it reads as light raking
         the terrain rather than a light floating in open sky. Tracks the
         sun's real horizontal position (--sun-az), so at a real sunrise
         it catches the terrain in the east and at sunset the west,
         rather than always lighting the middle of the line. -->
    <div id="horizon-rim-light" aria-hidden="true" class="absolute pointer-events-none"></div>

    <!-- THE NIGHT SKY: moon (real current phase, shown only when real
         moonrise/moonset math says it's actually above the horizon) + a
         real bright-star catalog (2026-08-02 rework — see applyStars()
         below). Empty on purpose: populated as small circular divs by JS,
         since real stars need real altitude/azimuth per star, and a
         fixed-shape SVG scaled to fit whatever width/height the header
         happens to be at could distort circles into ellipses; plain
         divs with a fixed pixel diameter stay round regardless.
         Precedes the content row in DOM order, which is load-bearing now
         that the stars share the sun's vertical band: the name, the nav
         and the land all paint in front of the field.

         STARS BEFORE MOON, both BEFORE THE SUN. Nothing in this group
         carries a z-index, so paint order is plain DOM order — later
         wins — and the whole sky reads back-to-front: stars, then moon,
         then sun, then the windmill and the land in front of all of it.

         Stars before moon (2026-08-10): the moon had been first, so the
         star field painted on top of it and a star behind the moon's own
         disc showed through.

         Night-sky before sun-wrap (2026-08-10, second pass): this block
         used to sit AFTER #sun-wrap, so the moon painted over the sun.
         That looked fine on desktop and broke on phones, and the reason
         is the sky window, not the stacking: the projection squeezes
         250deg of azimuth into the measured gap between the name and the
         nav, and on a narrow viewport measureSkyWindow() falls through to
         the MIN_SKY_SPAN_PCT floor of 12%. On a 390px phone that is ~47px
         of total travel for the entire sky, so bodies up to ~60deg apart
         land within a disc's width of each other — and 60deg of elongation
         is an ordinary crescent, not a rare alignment. The same 60deg is
         ~138px apart on a 1440px desktop, which is why it never showed
         there.

         Two bodies genuinely overlapping is a projection artifact rather
         than astronomy: the real moon only crosses the real sun at new
         moon, i.e. a solar eclipse. So the question is only which artifact
         looks less wrong, and the sun has to win — it is the brightest
         thing in the header and carries its own glow, while the moon at a
         near-solar elongation is by definition nearly new, which here means
         a disc filled almost entirely by the sky-coloured shadow mask. That
         is the exact case the old order rendered: a sky-coloured hole
         punched through the middle of the sun. -->
    <div id="night-sky" aria-hidden="true" class="absolute inset-0 pointer-events-none">
      <div id="night-stars" class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full pointer-events-none"></div>
      <div id="horizon-moon"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- THE SUN (dawn / day / dusk) -->
    <div id="sun-wrap" aria-hidden="true" class="absolute pointer-events-none">
      <!-- Grazing light: the aureole — the atmosphere's own forward-scattered
           glow around a low sun. Near-round on purpose; the horizontal
           spread a sunset appears to have belongs to the twilight arch
           below, not to this. See the long note in the stylesheet. -->
      <div id="sun-bloom"></div>
      <!-- Grazing light: a handful of uneven, mostly-horizontal rays —
           down from a symmetric 8-line starburst, since a low sun's
           visible rays fan out sideways along a flat horizon far more
           than straight up/down. Only visible when the sun sits low near
           the horizon (real elevation, same value driving --sun-elev) —
           fades out well before the sun climbs toward noon. -->
      <svg id="sun-rays" aria-hidden="true" class="absolute pointer-events-none" width="96" height="54" viewBox="-48 -27 96 54">
        <g stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round">
          <line x1="6"  y1="-0.5" x2="45"  y2="-3"  stroke-width="1"   opacity="0.55" />
          <line x1="-6" y1="0.5"  x2="-43" y2="3"   stroke-width="0.9" opacity="0.45" />
          <line x1="5"  y1="-2"   x2="26"  y2="-11" stroke-width="0.8" opacity="0.3"  />
          <line x1="-5" y1="1.5"  x2="-21" y2="10"  stroke-width="0.8" opacity="0.26" />
          <line x1="4"  y1="-1.5" x2="14"  y2="-15" stroke-width="0.6" opacity="0.18" />
